rs6000: keep the tree IV optimizer off by default in the cell64lv2 ILP32 ABI

Under ILP32 a pointer is 32 bits but addresses are formed from full 64-bit
registers.  IVOPTS biases an address IV one step below its object so an
update-form load (lfsu 4(rX)) reaches the first element; the bias (-4)
lives in a 32-bit register.  Spilled with stw and reloaded with lwz it
comes back zero-extended (0x00000000fffffffc), the full-width add then
carries into bit 32 and the access faults on hardware (RPCS3's PPU
interpreter reports a narrowing error; its LLVM recompiler truncates the
address and hides it).

Default -fno-ivopts when the data model is ILP32 and the user has not
chosen; an explicit -fivopts still enables it, and LP64 is unchanged.
Applied in rs6000_override_options_after_change so per-function optimize
attributes keep the default.  This is a mitigation: the address model
itself (32-bit Pmode on 64-bit hardware) is the cause.

--- a/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.cc
+++ b/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.cc
@@ -3464,6 +3464,19 @@
   /* If we are inserting ROP-protect instructions, disable shrink wrap.  */
   if (rs6000_rop_protect)
     flag_shrink_wrap = 0;
+
+#ifdef POWERPC_CELL64LV2
+  /* Under the ILP32 data model a pointer is 32 bits wide but address
+     arithmetic runs in 64-bit registers.  The tree IV optimizer builds
+     address IVs whose start is biased one step below the object (so an
+     update-form load reaches the first element); that biased offset lives
+     in a 32-bit register, and when it is spilled and reloaded with lwz it
+     comes back zero-extended, so the full-width address add carries into
+     bit 32 and the access faults.  Keep the pass off for ILP32 unless the
+     user asks for it explicitly.  */
+  if (!TARGET_LP64 && !OPTION_SET_P (flag_ivopts))
+    flag_ivopts = 0;
+#endif
 }
 
 #ifdef TARGET_USES_LINUX64_OPT
